WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
This is how you use AI to change your

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:09.680
life. Most of us use ChatGPT or Claude

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.120
for productivity hacks or [music] to do

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:16.000
work that we don't want to do. But can

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:19.080
AI actually make a difference? Can it

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.560
help you build a life worth waking up

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:25.120
for? I think any thinking partner can.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.680
If you use it the right way, so we're

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:31.800
going to look at the five blind spots

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:34.320
that keep you stuck in survival mode.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:37.200
For each blind spot, we're going to do

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:41.080
three things. First, unpack why it

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:43.320
happens. Second, show how AI can help.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:46.320
And third, what you can actually do

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:49.360
about each blind spot. And for each one,

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.760
I'll share a specific approach and a

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:55.800
specific prompt that turns AI into your

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:58.760
partner as you reinvent yourself. I'll

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:01.800
also show you where AI is limited and

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:05.000
why your judgment still has to stay in

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:08.960
charge. Do this seriously for just 30

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:12.200
days and you may become unrecognizable

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:14.280
in the only way that matters. The most

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.120
dangerous thing on the road is not

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:18.920
always the thing you can see in front of

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:22.400
you. On US highways, you see these

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:25.680
18-wheelers, these massive trucks that

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:28.720
weigh around 80,000 lb. They're very

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:32.160
hard to turn. Some personal injury law

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:36.400
firms estimate that 840,000

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:39.200
crashes a year in the US alone involve

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:40.120
lane changes and blind spots. And you

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.360
know,

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:46.520
our lives feel like those giant trucks

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:49.040
sometimes. The freight you carry is

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:53.400
heavy, building your career, your

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:55.480
family, a company, a future. The road is

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.480
full of lane changes.

00:01:55.480 --> 00:02:00.760
The first blind spot is [music]

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:02.840
deception. Why do I keep doing the thing

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:05.600
that I know is hurting me?

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:08.080
Every plane carries a device called the

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.119
black box. When something goes wrong,

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.720
investigators don't start with the

00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:15.320
pilot's memory or his or her story

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:18.440
because under pressure memory gets

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:20.560
distorted. They start with the data

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:22.120
recorded in the black box.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.720
Self-improvement

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:28.080
ought to start in the same place. Your

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:30.520
life is the black box. It has all the

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:33.280
data. It has all the information, but

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:35.840
you can make AI to act like the

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:37.600
investigator. It can help you examine

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.880
the evidence you can provide from a

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:42.400
third party's perspective. The pattern

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:44.800
that you may not have seen before become

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:47.920
more obvious. Here's our first prompt.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:50.680
Act as an investigator for my behavior.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:53.480
This is the pattern I keep repeating. I

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:55.840
don't give candid feedback to my team

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:59.360
members, even when I know it would help

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:01.920
them. I focus on positives and avoid the

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:04.400
uncomfortable conversations that could

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:07.560
help them improve dramatically.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:10.200
Now, this is a real pattern for me, but

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.239
you could insert any pattern of your

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.920
choice.

00:03:11.239 --> 00:03:16.760
For you, the pattern might be that you

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:20.239
can't work out consistently, which is my

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:22.720
problem, too. Or that um work keeps

00:03:20.239 --> 00:03:26.280
getting your best attention while your

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:28.720
partner or family gets the leftovers.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:31.200
Whatever it is, share here. Next part of

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:34.080
the prompt. I want to understand three

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:36.720
things. One, where my blind spots may

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:38.600
be. Two, why I keep avoiding them.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.840
Three, how do I catch them before

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:44.160
they're triggered? Interview me one

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:47.000
question at a time. Unpack my answer and

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:50.040
based on it, ask me the next better

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:52.440
question. Use only my answer as

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:54.840
evidence. When you're ready, give me

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:57.080
your best hypotheses, the evidence

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:59.960
behind it, and the evidence that's still

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:02.680
missing. Be respectful, candid, and

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:05.400
direct. Do not motivate me or flatter

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:07.120
me. Do not suck up to me.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:10.680
That's the whole move. That's the

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:14.200
prompt. One very important caveat here,

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:16.920
AI doesn't have any divine insights.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:20.000
It can distort facts, it can overstate

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:22.960
patterns, and it sounds more certain

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:27.400
than it should. So, treat the answer as

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:30.160
a hypothesis, not a diagnosis. AI is not

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:33.680
a trained psychologist. So, why use it

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:36.400
at all? Because your own mind

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:38.640
is not neutral, either. The problem is

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:41.320
that we rarely recognize our own

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:43.200
self-deception. Once we've found a story

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:45.880
that protects us,

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:47.040
we stick to it. We stop asking a harder

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:49.520
question.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:50.880
Now, what do you actually do in real

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:54.120
life when you see it spots such a

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.560
deception? A study from Frontiers in

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.919
Psychology found something very

00:04:55.560 --> 00:05:00.880
interesting. They found that real,

00:04:57.919 --> 00:05:03.000
concrete, negative feedback can reduce

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:05.320
self-deception. I was surprised to find

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:08.280
that, too. And I don't mean feedback

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:10.480
from social media trolls. Ignore all of

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:13.080
that. I mean feedback from someone who

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:15.800
has worked with you, who knows you well.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:18.400
That person has already told you the

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:20.880
truth at least once in the past. Here's

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:23.800
one thing that I do. I keep a list of

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:27.000
every hard piece of feedback I've gotten

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:30.080
over the years. And I read it sometimes.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:33.160
I go back to it. I don't kick myself for

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:36.080
it, but again, it's a good mirror.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:39.200
Also, if you want a more in-depth look

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:41.200
at topics like this one, please join our

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:44.440
newsletter. Every Tuesday, [music]

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:46.840
one insight, one tool, and one practice.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:49.800
I love writing it. Link is below, and

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:52.400
it's totally free. Now, let's go to the

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:55.520
second blind spot, relationship. The

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:58.560
stories we tell ourselves about others.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.520
There is a psychologist named Eli Finkel

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:05.120
who ran a study at Northwestern

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:06.640
University with 120 married couples. And

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:09.440
all of those couples had their

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.280
relationships slowly getting worse. Now,

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:13.640
some of these couples were asked to

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:16.080
write about their worst fights from a

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:17.840
view of a neutral outsider who wanted

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:20.000
the best for both of them. And you just

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:22.240
had to do it for 20-odd minutes. And the

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:25.000
surprising fact was [music] the decline

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:26.840
for those couples stopped. The rest who

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:29.000
didn't go through that writing exercise

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:31.120
kept sliding further. And I think that

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:34.480
happened because writing brought them

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:38.000
some clarity that every communication

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:40.200
has four parts. What you meant to say,

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.320
what came out of your mouth, what the

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:45.200
other person heard, and what they

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:47.600
interpreted. And all four of these can

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:50.440
be very different. Because in every

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:53.440
conversation, we bring a story. She

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:55.960
ignored me. He disrespected me. My

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:59.400
partner doesn't love me. My employees

00:06:55.960 --> 00:07:03.280
are lazy. On and on. And too often, the

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:05.160
goal of that conversation is to reaffirm

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:07.600
that story that's playing in our head.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:09.880
Every new detail is recruited to

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:12.440
reinforce that old story. And that's why

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:15.000
relationships are such powerful blind

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:16.680
spots. Because the distance between a

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:18.440
conversation

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:21.120
and a prosecution

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:23.680
is so tiny. This is where AI can help.

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:26.720
In the previous blind spot, we used AI

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:29.760
to act as an investigator. For this one,

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:32.880
we use AI as the counterparty.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:34.760
Use it to build the stronger version of

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:37.800
the other person's case.

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:40.120
Here's the prompt. Act as the person I

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:42.120
am in conflict with, or the person and

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:44.600
trying to communicate with. Do not

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:47.600
pretend that you know their inner life.

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:49.560
Here's the situation in my own words.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:51.600
You can now describe the conflict or a

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:54.680
conversation that you want to have. Be

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:57.000
as honest as you can. Here's what I plan

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:59.640
to say. Tell AI what you're trying to

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:02.480
say, your side of the equation. Do not

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:04.960
take my side. Argue their case as they

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:06.840
might, assuming they're smart and fair

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:09.800
and have reasons that make sense to

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:12.440
them. Then, tell me the following. One,

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:15.800
what they most likely feel that I'm not

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:18.720
seeing. Two, the weakest part of my

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:21.640
argument. And three, what they may need

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:23.960
to hear from me. Do not flatter me. Do

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:25.760
not turn this into a therapy session.

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:27.400
Help me rehearse the actual

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:29.919
conversation.

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:32.280
That's the prompt. And by the way, for

00:08:29.919 --> 00:08:34.760
this one, turn the sound mode on and

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:37.159
have an actual spoken conversation with

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:39.479
AI. Now, what do we do about this in

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:42.320
real life? Well, the study that I talked

00:08:39.479 --> 00:08:44.800
about gave us a clue. That small habit

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:45.971
of taking a neutral, third-party

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:46.000
perspective

00:08:45.971 --> 00:08:47.839
>> [music]

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:50.080
>> helped stop the decline in those

00:08:47.839 --> 00:08:53.040
couples. So, that's definitely a good

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:55.141
move. One other thing I learned, a

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:58.280
little late in my life, unfortunately,

00:08:55.141 --> 00:09:00.280
[music] was to replace accusation with

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:01.880
observation. You know, we sometimes

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:04.360
don't know the difference between

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:06.000
attacking the pattern versus attacking

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:09.240
the person.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:12.720
The useful move is much simpler.

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:15.680
Name the behavior, name the impact,

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.960
and then ask the question. Be curious.

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:19.320
So, instead of saying, "You don't care

00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:20.480
about me."

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:23.400
try this.

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:25.480
You know, when I don't hear back from

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:28.640
you for 2 days,

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:30.760
I start wondering if this is important

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:32.440
to you. Is that fair?

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:34.640
Am I being [music] fair?

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:36.839
What do you think? That's the human work

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:39.160
that we have to do. AI can help you

00:09:36.839 --> 00:09:41.800
unpack the story, but you still have to

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:43.920
face that person yourself in that room.

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:46.440
Now, the third blind spot is the

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:49.880
relationship you have with your own

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:53.200
identity. We're entering an era where

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:55.200
you may have to reinvent yourself again

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:57.480
and again, and that's one of the

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:59.680
trickiest transformations.

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:01.360
That's where we go next. There is a

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:03.520
strange illusion in [clears throat]

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:06.440
psychology called the end of history

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:10.120
illusion. Researchers studied more than

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:13.040
19,000 people between the ages of 18 and

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:15.840
68, and they found that people can look

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:17.800
backward and see how much they have

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:19.000
changed, but when they start looking

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:20.480
forward,

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:22.920
they assume they're not going to change

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:24.760
that much. We know how much we changed

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:27.440
in the last 5 years,

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:29.320
but we feel certain we'll be the same

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:32.000
for the next 5 years.

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:34.680
As if we have arrived at some final

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:36.480
version of who we are.

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:39.880
That's never the case.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:42.640
That is the identity blind spot, and it

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:45.440
gets worse. Sports researchers have

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:48.560
found that athletes who have their sense

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:49.880
of self fused too tightly with the sport

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:52.160
they play

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:55.640
often have the hardest time when they

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:58.200
retire. And you know, I have had a lot

00:10:55.640 --> 00:11:01.000
of personal experience with this idea of

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:03.240
ever-changing identity. I have pivoted

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:05.440
so many times in my life. When I was

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:06.680
young, I wanted to be a monk. That's all

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:09.680
I wanted to be.

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:12.000
I trained for it in an ashram. Well,

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:13.680
that didn't work out. Then I tried to be

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:16.720
a full-time musician, then a

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:20.280
mathematician, an equity analyst on Wall

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:23.520
Street, a CEO of an AI company, a board

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:26.120
member, then an investor, and now this

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:29.280
YouTube channel. So, technically I am a

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:32.960
creator, but now when I look back and

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:35.720
think about it, none of those roles were

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:38.920
fused with my identity. They didn't form

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:40.400
my identity. They were just clothes that

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:43.320
I was wearing,

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:45.640
not my skin. I wore them when it was

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:49.280
time, I took them off.

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:52.040
So, that's one good way to look at life.

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:55.200
The confusion feels existential, but

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:58.200
it's just navigational. This is where AI

00:11:55.200 --> 00:12:00.920
can help. For this blind spot, AI is

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:03.560
your mapmaker. It can give you a map

00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:06.240
from the old territory to the new

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:08.560
landscape. Here's the prompt. Act as my

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:12.160
transformation partner. You are my

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:14.640
mapmaker. Map out my next move. Here's

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:17.040
where I am right now. So, here you can

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:19.840
put your current role, your strengths,

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:21.640
your habits, your fears, what people

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:23.680
reward you for, and so on and so forth.

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:25.400
Give it more details. Here's where I'm

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:27.160
trying to go.

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:29.480
Here you'll describe your new chapter.

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:31.960
Be honest, you know, your new job, your

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:34.960
new company, new career, new

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:37.600
relationship, new phase of life, health

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:40.800
reset, whatever lies ahead.

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:42.839
Now, interview me one question at a time

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:45.520
to find the gap. Then, give me an action

00:12:42.839 --> 00:12:48.560
plan and a map that bridges that gap.

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:51.720
Respond to these five questions. Number

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:54.520
one, which of my strengths still will

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:56.600
carry over? Number two, which strengths

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:59.000
will become liabilities in this new

00:12:56.600 --> 00:13:01.360
chapter? Number three, what I need to

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:04.000
leave behind even though it's worked

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:06.560
before? Number four, what I've never

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:09.520
built but is now needed? And number

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:12.120
five, what I should test for at least 30

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:14.800
days before I make a bigger commitment.

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:17.160
Do not give me abstract purpose advice.

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:20.000
Do not flatter me. Do not give me a

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:22.400
10-year life plan. Give me a practical

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:23.720
transformation map. And do not suck up

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:25.640
to me.

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:27.160
You have to keep saying that.

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:31.160
All right, so that was our prompt.

00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:34.280
Again, one caveat, AI won't know

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:37.200
the emotional cost of whatever you're

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:39.760
leaving behind to become someone new. It

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:43.000
will make this reinvention of your

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:46.600
identity sound crisp and clean. But,

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:49.160
life is usually pretty messy. I've gone

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:52.240
through such transformations many times,

00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:53.720
and I know there is grief,

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:56.040
there is fear,

00:13:53.720 --> 00:13:58.880
there is anxiety.

00:13:56.040 --> 00:14:01.240
But, there's also growth. Now, what do

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:04.000
you actually do once you see the gap?

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:06.400
You should run the smallest real test

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:08.560
that you can. For instance, don't quit

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:12.000
your day job until your new adventure

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:14.960
shows some promising signs of working.

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:17.920
The fourth blind spot is forks, the

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:20.080
decision you keep staring at because

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:23.400
it's hard to make. There is a movie

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:26.280
called Cast Away. And at the end of the

00:14:23.400 --> 00:14:29.280
movie, the main character, played by Tom

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:32.800
Hanks, is standing in the middle of a

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:35.760
crossroad, a map in hand, weighing his

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:38.839
options. We've seen everything that he's

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:41.760
gone through and what brought him here.

00:14:38.839 --> 00:14:44.920
And for a moment, the whole movie boils

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:47.600
down to one question, which road will he

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:49.480
take? We all know that feeling,

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:52.120
standing at a fork,

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:56.200
staring at a decision we don't know how

00:14:52.120 --> 00:15:00.800
to make. Do I go left or right? Do I

00:14:56.200 --> 00:15:04.160
quit or stay? Propose or wait?

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:07.200
Forks are hard because both roads have a

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:09.200
cost. Both roads have a risk, and you

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:11.760
don't get to live both of them

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:14.520
simultaneously. So, how do you forecast

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:17.360
these risks? Gary Klein created a

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:19.240
decision tool called pre-mortem. Before

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:22.200
you make the decision, you should

00:15:19.240 --> 00:15:24.640
imagine the absolute worst scenario.

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:27.320
Describe it in detail. Write it down.

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:30.240
Then ask, "What went wrong? What did I

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:33.000
ignore? What was I too excited to miss?

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:35.200
What was I too afraid to admit?" This is

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:37.520
all hypothetical, so create the most

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:39.840
realistic scenario you can. And this is

00:15:37.520 --> 00:15:42.080
where AI can help you tremendously. In

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:44.960
this blind spot, AI becomes your

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:47.840
simulator. It runs that pre-mortem with

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:51.040
you. And here's the prompt. "Act as my

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:51.840
decision simulator. I am facing a fork

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:53.440
ahead."

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:56.360
Here you can describe the decision you

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:58.520
have to take, the options you have, etc.

00:15:56.360 --> 00:16:01.560
Option A is

00:15:58.520 --> 00:16:04.400
describe option A. Option B is

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:06.760
describe option B. Here's what I know.

00:16:04.400 --> 00:16:09.920
Now you can add all the constraints,

00:16:06.760 --> 00:16:11.960
money, time, people, organization,

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:14.280
timeline, whatever. The rest is the

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:16.960
same. Interview me one question at a

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:20.040
time to understand the decision, and

00:16:16.960 --> 00:16:23.080
then run a separate pre-mortem on each

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:25.960
option. For each one, imagine that it's

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:28.800
2 years later and that choice failed

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:31.320
badly, and work backward from there, and

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:34.080
tell me why. Treat them independently,

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:36.360
so I can compare how each road fails

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:38.600
differently. And then

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:41.880
for each road, tell me these three

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:44.280
things. Number one, the most likely way

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:47.080
it will fail. Number two, the warning

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:50.680
signs I should see first. And number

00:16:47.080 --> 00:16:53.480
three, the cost I cannot reverse. Do not

00:16:50.680 --> 00:16:57.280
tell me what to choose. Be very rigorous

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:58.920
and systematic about your analysis.

00:16:57.280 --> 00:17:01.080
All right. So, that was the prompt.

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:03.400
Again, you can add anything you want on

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:06.319
top of it. You're not asking AI to

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:09.120
choose the road for you. It cannot. No

00:17:06.319 --> 00:17:12.480
one can. But AI can pressure test your

00:17:09.120 --> 00:17:14.560
assumptions. It can surface risks that

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:16.480
you haven't thought about yet, and help

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:18.959
you understand the trade-offs. That's

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:20.880
what that exercise is for. Now, what do

00:17:18.959 --> 00:17:25.000
you do in real life after AI has

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:27.400
analyzed it? I like Jeff Bezos' rule. He

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:30.280
talks about two types of decisions,

00:17:27.400 --> 00:17:32.600
one-way doors and two-way doors. A

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:34.600
two-way door is when you can walk

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:36.680
through a door and if it doesn't work

00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:39.040
out, you walk back.

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:42.000
The cost is bearable.

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:44.640
In those cases, you should move fast.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:45.840
And a one-way door is the one you can't

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:49.600
reverse.

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:51.720
Those are rare, but they do happen.

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:53.160
When you're facing one of those,

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:56.480
slow down.

00:17:53.160 --> 00:18:00.440
Now comes the fifth blind spot, trials.

00:17:56.480 --> 00:18:03.000
What do you do with the trauma and pain

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:05.400
after you have failed? Our trials and

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:07.720
traumas don't ask for our permission.

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:09.800
They arrive unannounced.

00:18:07.720 --> 00:18:11.720
They don't care for our opinions.

00:18:09.800 --> 00:18:14.120
Long-term viewers know this story that

00:18:11.720 --> 00:18:17.840
I've shared before. At one of my jobs,

00:18:14.120 --> 00:18:20.720
my boss invited me to a very fancy

00:18:17.840 --> 00:18:22.480
restaurant for drinks and dinner. And I

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:24.760
was thinking that I was getting

00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:27.840
promoted. And in the middle of the

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:29.160
dinner, he handed me a brown envelope

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:31.360
and said,

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:35.600
"We've decided to let you go."

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:38.440
When failure comes this suddenly, your

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:40.680
entire body just shuts down. But the

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:43.880
blind spot isn't the event. It's the

00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:47.200
story that stays behind. Why do I keep

00:18:43.880 --> 00:18:49.960
screwing up? I shouldn't trust people. I

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:54.160
should have never tried that. On and on

00:18:49.960 --> 00:18:56.440
and on. A researcher at UT Austin found

00:18:54.160 --> 00:18:59.440
something strange. People who wrote

00:18:56.440 --> 00:19:02.360
about a painful experience for even 15

00:18:59.440 --> 00:19:04.840
minutes four days running were way

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:07.360
healthier months later. And that has

00:19:04.840 --> 00:19:08.520
been my experience, too. When [music] we

00:19:07.360 --> 00:19:11.520
fall,

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:15.040
writing about it helps a lot. And this

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:18.520
is why AI can help. For this blind spot,

00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:21.760
AI becomes your debrief partner, a

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:24.720
companion. Here's the prompt. Act as a

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:26.400
debrief partner for something difficult

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:28.320
that I have gone through. Here's what

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:29.600
happened. Now you can describe the

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:30.880
failure,

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:35.440
loss,

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:37.040
your setback, betrayal, rejection, grief

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:39.640
in plain language.

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:42.400
The story I've been telling myself about

00:19:39.640 --> 00:19:44.840
this is this. Share what you've been

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:49.400
thinking about, about yourself, about

00:19:44.840 --> 00:19:52.920
other people, trust, risk, love, work,

00:19:49.400 --> 00:19:55.840
future, all of it. The rest, of course,

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:58.600
is the same. Interview me one question

00:19:55.840 --> 00:20:00.840
at a time. Do not comfort me or try to

00:19:58.600 --> 00:20:03.320
find a solution. I just [music] want to

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:05.720
talk. And then help me understand the

00:20:03.320 --> 00:20:09.040
following four things. Number one, the

00:20:05.720 --> 00:20:12.120
facts of what happened. Number two, the

00:20:09.040 --> 00:20:13.800
meaning I keep attributing that isn't

00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:16.680
about the facts. [clears throat] Number

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:19.160
three, what was mine to control

00:20:16.680 --> 00:20:21.600
and what wasn't. And number four, one

00:20:19.160 --> 00:20:23.160
thing to carry forward, one thing to

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:25.680
leave behind.

00:20:23.160 --> 00:20:28.840
That's the prompt. And again, remember,

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:33.280
AI is not a therapist.

00:20:28.840 --> 00:20:36.360
And for some traumas and some trials,

00:20:33.280 --> 00:20:37.800
you're going to need one. If this is a

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:39.200
grief,

00:20:37.800 --> 00:20:42.440
pain,

00:20:39.200 --> 00:20:44.800
or a weight you can't lift alone, a

00:20:42.440 --> 00:20:48.360
language model is not the way to go.

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:50.160
Talk to a close friend or an expert.

00:20:48.360 --> 00:20:53.040
Take care of yourself.

00:20:50.160 --> 00:20:56.800
Loss and grief change you.

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:59.000
They rearrange you forever.

00:20:56.800 --> 00:21:00.480
You don't get to go back to who you were

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:03.080
before.

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:05.840
But you don't have to let your wound

00:21:03.080 --> 00:21:09.280
become your way of life.

00:21:05.840 --> 00:21:10.760
There is an old Buddhist teaching

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:12.920
that [music]

00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:16.000
pain and suffering

00:21:12.920 --> 00:21:17.080
are not the same things.

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:18.640
Pain

00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:21.840
is what happened.

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:24.280
Suffering is the story we keep telling

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:27.720
ourselves after.

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:30.160
You know, when I first learned guitar,

00:21:27.720 --> 00:21:33.640
the skin on my fingertips

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:34.880
would split. It would bleed. It hurt.

00:21:33.640 --> 00:21:37.160
But then

00:21:34.880 --> 00:21:39.720
the skin hardened.

00:21:37.160 --> 00:21:42.000
First, there's pain.

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:43.320
Then there's music.

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:45.960
Thank you.

00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:45.960
And I love you.

